 ## Overview
-A rendering method that uses per-point 3D covariance estimated from k-nearest neighbours to project an elliptical weighted average (EWA) shape onto the 2D image plane for denser coverage than simple disk splatting [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from fixed-radius EWA splatting by adapting the kernel footprint to local point density, reducing holes in sparse regions and preventing excessive overlap in dense regions [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) by providing a data-driven covariance estimation step prior to the standard EWA projection [S1, unofficial].
+A rendering method that uses per-point 3D covariance estimated from k-nearest neighbours to project an elliptical weighted average (EWA) shape onto the 2D image plane for denser coverage than simple disk splatting [S1, unofficial]. It distinguishes itself from standard [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) by deriving the local surface geometry and anisotropy directly from the point cloud's neighbourhood structure rather than assuming isotropic Gaussians or fixed kernel shapes, allowing it to handle raw, unstructured point clouds without mesh reconstruction [S1, unofficial].
 
